{
  "image_name": "14f0bd1f46d82f11b4bee3603cf7c004.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how stimulus-responsive chromophores can control and read out multiexciton and triplet spin states in singlet-fission systems. The top section contrasts molecular arrangements with small versus large electronic coupling, shown as orthogonal versus aligned chromophores and as conjugation switched off versus on. It also illustrates that excited-state behavior can be tuned by environmental factors such as viscosity and by mechanically restricting molecular motion, which alters fluctuations in zero-field splitting and changes whether the system is flexible and variable or rigid and fixed.\n\nThe middle section shows an optical strategy for preparing and detecting triplet pairs. A linked dimer first forms correlated triplet states through singlet fission, then evolves under double intersystem crossing and selective triplet loss to independent triplets. Closing a molecular switch appears to generate a spin-correlated multiexciton state, and if spin quantum numbers satisfy a condition, triplet–triplet annihilation gives an optical readout by returning population to a singlet emissive state.\n\nThe bottom section outlines mechanisms for multicolor control: sequential two-color singlet fission, virtual-state-mediated two-color excitation, and a two-color triplet–triplet annihilation scheme involving energy transfer and intersystem crossing, allowing different wavelengths to address distinct steps in the multiexciton cycle.",
